# GitHub Issues

## Before substantive implementation

1. Check related issues (`gh issue list`; `gh issue view N` when relevant) for substantive, tracked work.
2. Prefer extending an existing issue over creating a duplicate.
3. Align implementation with issue acceptance criteria when an issue exists.

Tiny, self-contained fixes and read-only tasks do not need issue-tracker ceremony. Follow explicit user requests and other project policies when they require an issue.

## PRs

- Done → `Closes #N` · Partial/related → `Refs #N`

## Issue closure gate

Before adding `Closes #N` or manually closing an issue:

1. Read the issue's current acceptance criteria, including later clarifications. Make any ambiguous or untestable criterion concrete with the issue owner before treating it as complete.
2. Check the implementation that will be merged (or is already merged) against **each criterion**. Record a criterion-to-evidence mapping in the PR description or issue: identify the implementation and the check or direct observation that demonstrates it. A passing build or test suite alone does not establish criteria it does not exercise.
3. Verify user-visible criteria through the actual user-facing path on every host or environment named by the criterion. Source code, bundled assets, and screenshots from a different host are not evidence that the required path works.

Use `Closes #N` only when the implementation being merged satisfies every criterion and the evidence is recorded. For partial work, missing evidence, or a known gap, use `Refs #N` and leave the issue open; update its criteria or record the gap and follow-up when scope changes. Manually close only after the satisfying implementation is available on the target branch and the same evidence is recorded.

## Gaps discovered mid-work

Update issue body/checklist, comment, or reopen — do not silently diverge from the tracker.
